// Generated from skills/ by scripts/package-skills.mjs.
export const skillFiles: Record<string,string> = {
  "contextory-codebase/references/retrieval.md": "<!-- contextory-managed-resource -->\n\n# Retrieval and coverage\n\n## Hook recovery\n\nUse `contextory_hooks_rebuild` only for an intended project's missing, stale or duplicate managed hooks: `{ \"projectId\": \"<project>\", \"provider\": \"codex\", \"operationId\": \"<new unique ID>\" }`. Supported providers are `codex`, `claude` and `bob`; Generic MCP has no hooks. The response separates changed hook files, launcher restoration and current-service MCP/hook observations. It never reads or writes native approval records, global hooks, MCP configuration or portable skills. Keep the exact operation ID/input for an interrupted retry; a later repair needs a new ID. Project/workspace scope is unchanged and reference codebases cannot be repaired through this tool.\n\nIf files are unchanged but hooks remain unobserved, review native trust and reload the affected session instead of issuing more rebuilds. The installed Codex client may distinguish `C:\\\\` and `c:\\\\` when looking up hook approval: use `/hooks` from the affected session's actual project path. When MCP is unavailable, the editor's **Contextory: Rebuild Agent Hooks** command or sidebar **Rebuild hooks** action can initiate recovery after the local service starts. Inspect service output if startup itself fails; rebuilding through MCP requires a reachable service.\n\n## Choose the lookup\n\n| Need                                                | Tool and useful inputs                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| --------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Workspace contracts, active work, configured checks | `contextory_context_get`, optionally `specId`. This brief is cached orientation, not a fresh source investigation.                                                                                                                                                     |\n| Locate code for a task                              | `contextory_code_context_get` with `query`, known `paths`, optional `specId` and `budgetTokens` (512–6000, default 3000). Exact definitions rank before mentions; neighborhoods and linked project records are bounded.                                                |\n| Understand subsystems and entry points              | `contextory_code_architecture_get`. Default `refresh:true` verifies saved source; use `refresh:false` only when a cached overview is sufficient.                                                                                                                       |\n| Find callers, dependencies, tests or linked work    | `contextory_impact_get` with `id`, `direction` (`reverse` for dependents, `forward` for dependencies, `both` for surrounding relationships), `depth` and `limit`.                                                                                                      |\n| Explain how two things connect                      | `contextory_code_path_get` with `from`, `to`, optional `direction` (`forward` or `both`) and `maxDepth`. Returned edges retain their original direction.                                                                                                               |\n| Inspect calls or branches                           | `contextory_flow_trace` with `root:{path,symbol}` or a zero-based position, `view: calls/flow`, `direction: outgoing/incoming`, and optional limits. Supported engines cover TS/JS and Python.                                                                         |\n| Recover earlier intent and rationale                | `contextory_search` with a focused `query`, `mode` and `limit`; inspect full records with `contextory_entity_get`, including a cited `revision` when checking history. Verify current state before relying on an old decision.                                         |\n| Continue a bounded result                           | `contextory_entity_get` uses `offset`/`limit` for children, job results and 400-character artifact pages. `contextory_history_get` uses the returned `nextBefore` as `before`. Flow pages/expansion use `contextory_flow_expand` with the original graph/snapshot IDs. |\n\nFor example, a task lookup can use `{\"query\":\"How do provider connections install skills?\",\"paths\":[\"src/providers\"],\"budgetTokens\":2000}`. Follow it with impact on the cited definition and inspect its current file and relevant callers. Avoid assuming this example's paths exist in another project.\n\nSource tools accept file paths, unique names or `source:path#qualifiedName` where their schemas permit. Choose a path-qualified candidate when a name is ambiguous. A failed search or absent bounded path does not prove two components are independent. Inspect category, evidence locations and provenance for each material edge. Semantic unavailability has an explicit lexical fallback in hybrid search; semantic-only search may fail. Narrow query/path scope or page results when truncated instead of presenting a bounded slice as exhaustive.\n\n## Freshness and coverage\n\nSaved file create/edit/delete/rename, Git changes and configuration changes trigger debounced refresh, backed by polling. Task context, impact and path queries also verify disk content before answering. Re-query after relevant saves; do not request a full manual reindex after every edit. Source context describes saved files. Only trusted editor flow requests can carry unsaved overlays; their snapshots are separate and expire when documents change.\n\nFor an empty/stale/failed index, inspect status/errors and use `contextory_code_index_refresh` when a retry can help. For partial coverage, inspect the actual gaps: file/byte bounds may call for narrower reviewed configuration, while dynamic dispatch, callbacks and external code require native inspection. Repeated refresh does not create missing language support or execution evidence.\n\nNative extraction covers TS/JS through its compiler and Python through isolated AST parsing. Markdown links and recognized configuration paths add explicit connections. Graphify JSON adds other language facts with supplied provenance. Changed cited source invalidates imported facts: regenerate the export and re-import using `contextory_code_index_refresh` with a workspace-relative `graphifyPath`; refresh alone cannot regenerate external analysis. Static calls, inferred message links and test references do not prove execution or coverage.\n\n`contextory.project.json` holds reviewed configuration: schemaVersion 1, optional name, include/exclude patterns, entryPoints, conventions, maxFiles and maxBytes. Generated topology lives in rebuildable local storage; it does not approve requirements or overwrite authoritative work records. Consult user instructions and current decisions before treating a discovered convention as a constraint.\n\n## Reference codebases\n\n### Active workspace projects\n\nTrusted local multi-root workspaces attach up to sixteen independent folder services. `contextory_workspace_get` lists public project IDs, names and canonical roots, plus attached workspace contexts; it never returns credential paths. All tools accept `projectId` and `workspaceId` as common scope. Selection applies only to that call. Omitting projectId/current stays on the server's project, regardless of the editor's selected project or earlier calls. Multiple attached editor contexts require workspaceId for cross-project operations. Unknown, detached or identity-changed projects fail; do not retry against a different project. Use the same projectId when reading returned record IDs, artifacts or graph pages. Keep the returned project identity with relative source paths and symbols. Read-only lookups never merge project memory.\n\nThe editor project selector scopes Requests, Knowledge, History, Versions, Architecture, checks and source navigation together; requests and specs carry project badges. Each folder retains its existing root-derived storage, ledger, graph, catalog, references, releases and checks. Removing a folder detaches its routing without deleting data. Shared/native hook observations stay with their observing project; usage is not inferred across project leases. A workspace is not a new merged project.\n\nThe CLI `projects [group-id] --descriptor <generic.json>` reads active membership or a coordinated request; `--project <id>` and `--workspace <id>` scope other CLI calls. A standalone service has only its local project until an editor attaches membership. Do not use codebaseId or a reference registration to authorize cross-project work. Coordinated requests use the workflow skill's project-owned branches.\n\nWhen advertised, `contextory_codebase_list` returns the active workspace (`current`) and up to eight named local references. Register only a directory within the user's requested scope using `contextory_codebase_change` with `action:\"add\"`, an absolute `root`, optional `name`, and a new `operationId`. Registration resolves aliases and persists in the current service storage; it does not modify the reference. Detach using `action:\"remove\"`, its `codebaseId` and a new operation ID. Cached projections remain rebuildable service data.\n\nPass that ID as `codebaseId` on architecture, code context, search, impact, path, index/import and flow trace/expand calls. Paths, Graphify input and source IDs resolve inside that reference. References cannot use current-project `specId` or workflow mutations. Omission or `current` always means the active workspace, even after another reference query. Separate requests can analyze different references concurrently; there is no global project switch or inferred cross-repository relationship. Preserve each response's `codebase`, snapshot, coverage and citations when combining an explanation. A same-path/same-name symbol in two repositories is not the same source.\n\nReference context/impact/path/search and default architecture queries verify saved files on demand. References are not watched in the background. `refresh:false` explicitly uses cached architecture; use the default refresh after edits, and regenerate/re-import stale Graphify facts within the selected reference. Source files are parsed without executing them. Existing include/exclude/configuration and language coverage rules apply separately to each root. Reference search uses lexical graph matching (hybrid reports fallback); semantic-only reference search is unavailable. No active-project decisions, requirements, checks or unsaved editor overlays are imported into reference analysis.\n\nCLI equivalents are `references`, `reference-add <folder> [--name <label>]`, `reference-remove <id>`, and source commands with `--codebase <id>`. Always keep `--root`/storage/descriptor on the current project whose reference registry you intend to use. Architecture in the editor offers **Add reference**, a **Codebase** selector and **Detach reference**; source navigation stays inside the selected root. Applying any learned behavior to the current application remains a separate user-authorized implementation task.\n\n## Clients without a capability\n\nUse only the tools advertised in the current session. After an extension upgrade, an existing connection may still expose older tools until the client reloads its MCP connection. Continue useful work with available search/flow tools, the packaged CLI, or native `rg`/file reads; state the missing graph coverage when it affects conclusions. Hooks are advisory and optional.\n\nRun `node <installation>/dist/contextory.mjs help` for current CLI inputs. `context`, `impact`, `path`, `architecture` and `index` return JSON; `mcp` starts a hook-free stdio server. Supply the intended `--root` and either the existing service `--descriptor` or deliberately chosen `--storage` so the client reads the intended workspace and ledger. CLI code lookups do not replace MCP workflow mutations. Preserve the user's task and the host's permissions.\n\n## Application definitions and documentation\n\nUse `contextory_catalog_get` for file purposes, rules/features, UI placement/interactions, declared tests, pending proposals and source freshness. Record/proposal/inventory cursors are independent; keep paging each until exhausted. `contextory_project_export` overview enumerates stored documentation datasets; page current records, immutable revisions, history, relationships, source facts, jobs, usage metadata and artifacts with `offset`/`limit`. Keep sequence/snapshot and observed/approved/retired state. Use exact revisions for historical docs and current source for current behavior. The export grants no approval and exposes no service credentials or native conversations. Large result envelopes are read with `contextory_entity_get`.\n",
  "contextory-codebase/SKILL.md": "---\nname: contextory-codebase\ndescription: Understand a connected workspace, locate definitions and related code, assess source impact, and recover relevant project decisions through Contextory. Use for codebase questions and before changes spanning related components.\nmetadata:\n  version: \"9\"\n---\n\n<!-- contextory-managed-codebase -->\n\nUse the Contextory capabilities advertised by the current client. A connected MCP server or packaged CLI supplies shared retrieval; hooks and editor language services are optional. The same workflow applies in any agent.\n\nWhen authoring UI-facing project records, read `uiLanguage` in `contextory_context_get` and use that VS Code language (`en`/`pt-BR`) for all new prose, even if chat uses another language. Preserve stored historical text, source identifiers and code; bilingual documentation keeps its paired locales.\n\nIn a multi-root editor workspace, first use `contextory_workspace_get`. Pass the intended `projectId` on every related retrieval and workflow call, with `workspaceId` when multiple editor contexts are attached. Omission/current stays on that server's project; changing the UI project does not change agent defaults. Keep project identity/root with records, artifact handles, graph snapshots and citations. Identical paths/symbols in different projects are distinct. Unknown or ambiguous scope fails instead of selecting another project. `codebaseId` still selects a read-only reference within the chosen project; references are not writable workspace members.\n\nAfter resuming a chat, use its current advertised tools and a read-only probe before reporting Contextory disconnected; an earlier message or generated configuration does not establish current reachability. Report the concrete error or missing capability. In an editor, **Contextory: Check Native Connections** distinguishes current MCP/hook observations from history, and **Contextory: Restart Agent Extensions** reloads the Extension Host. Native project/hook trust remains with the client; reopening a session may still be required after tool changes.\n\nFor missing, stale or duplicate Contextory hooks, use advertised `contextory_hooks_rebuild` with the intended `projectId`, explicit native `provider` (`codex`, `claude`, `bob`) and a new `operationId`. It repairs managed hooks and the launcher without changing MCP configuration, other hooks or native trust. Inspect changed files and observations; an unchanged result is not proof of execution. Reuse the exact operation ID/input only to recover an interrupted call. If hooks remain unobserved after one repair, inspect native trust and service logs instead of repeatedly rebuilding. Codex approvals can differ for `C:` versus `c:`: review `/hooks` in the affected session using its actual workspace path. If MCP is unreachable, use **Contextory: Rebuild Agent Hooks** in the editor, then resolve any service startup error and reload the native session as needed. A rebuild cannot approve hooks or repair an unreachable service through MCP.\n\nStart a code question or related edit with `contextory_code_context_get`: put the actual task in `query`, scope with known `paths` and optionally `specId`, and choose a bounded `budgetTokens`. Read the cited current files before deciding or editing. Read-only exploration does not require a tracked request.\n\nUse `contextory_code_architecture_get` for orientation, `contextory_impact_get` for affected callers/dependencies/tests/records, `contextory_code_path_get` to explain a connection, and `contextory_flow_trace` for detailed branches/calls. Use `contextory_search` for earlier decisions, findings and changes, then inspect record state, revision and provenance. Resolve ambiguous symbols with `source:path#qualifiedName` where supported.\n\nWhen something stopped working, recover its catalog contract and last known working source/check/version, then follow impact links, catalog before/after revisions (including agent auto-approvals), decisions and spec history. Search by feature and source path/symbol and page chronology. Treat these as candidate causes until reproduced; Git version membership and ledger recording intervals are different evidence. Link a confirmed regression/fix to the responsible change/spec/source and retain uncertainty otherwise. Compatible maintenance and implementation conflicts may be agent auto-approved; process/outcome conflicts require human review before implementation. Approval provenance never substitutes for executed checks.\n\nKeep source citations, graph snapshot, coverage and truncation attached to conclusions. Narrow or page bounded results. Static calls, test references and inferred contracts are possible relationships, not proof of execution or test coverage. Generated facts do not become approved requirements.\n\nUse advertised `contextory_catalog_get` alongside source retrieval to recover file responsibilities, rules/features, test links and UI layout/interaction contracts; inspect state, revision, pending changes and source freshness. Page `inventory:true` to identify files without descriptions. For documentation, `contextory_project_export` overview discovers all stored documentation datasets, with independent pagination for records, immutable revisions, history, relationships, source graph, checks/jobs, usage metadata and artifacts. Retain sequence/snapshot and provenance, and distinguish current approved behavior from observations and historical definitions. Read-only documentation needs no tracked request.\n\nWhen advertised, `contextory_docs_get section=sections` retrieves authored project explanations by query, source path or local record ID, with stable page/section IDs, source freshness and observed associations. Follow `nextOffset` for coverage. Relevant explanations may accompany current-project code context; they remain separate from source facts and human-approved requirements. Use the `contextory-documentation` skill for authoring, migration, generation and maintenance with the bundled documentation/Carbon runtime. No separately installed plugin is required, and optional generation must not gate source retrieval or service startup.\n\nFor another local repository, use advertised `contextory_codebase_list` and `contextory_codebase_change` to register an authorized reference directory. Pass its `codebaseId` explicitly to source queries and flow expansion; omission/current always selects the active project. Reference graphs and project memory stay separate. Keep the returned codebase identity/root with relative citations. Analyzing a reference does not authorize applying its design, changing current requirements or editing either project. See the reference guide below for scope and freshness details.\n\nSaved edits refresh the source graph automatically; re-query relevant context after saving changes. Task/impact/path queries also verify disk content. Use `contextory_code_index_refresh` to recover an empty/stale/failed index or import regenerated Graphify data; repeated refresh cannot resolve every dynamic/external gap. Unsaved editor flow overlays are separate from the saved-file graph. If a capability is missing, use the packaged CLI or native source tools and state the gap; do not invent tool calls. Preserve the user's scope and native permissions.\n\nRead [retrieval and coverage](references/retrieval.md) for tool inputs, paging, freshness recovery and headless use.\n\nFor version-specific documentation, use advertised `contextory_version_get` and page each release's commits and semantic entries. Release entities and immutable revisions are also exported with project records. Git membership establishes which commits belong to a version; definitions/decisions associated by recording interval need that qualification. Local observed tags do not prove remote publication. This history is project-wide and needs no request/spec scope.\n",
  "contextory-documentation/SKILL.md": "---\nname: contextory-documentation\ndescription: Author, update, retrieve and build source-backed bilingual project documentation through Contextory's bundled software-documentation and Carbon runtime, including source and tracked-change associations.\nmetadata:\n  version: \"2\"\n---\n\n<!-- contextory-managed-documentation -->\n\nUse the existing Contextory MCP connection or packaged CLI. The documentation runtime, documentation playbooks, complete Carbon module, fonts and assets ship with Contextory; no CustomAgents/plugin installation or extra MCP registration is needed. Python 3.11+ is needed only for generator operations, using the project's `contextory.pythonExecutable`. Reading explanations and guidance does not require Python. Optional official Carbon MCP guidance is included; use it only if its tools are actually advertised.\n\nSelect the intended project with `contextory_workspace_get` and carry its `projectId` on every call. Read current task/source context and catalog before authoring. Page the relevant `contextory_project_export` datasets when documenting decisions, definitions, architecture, changes or evidence; retain record IDs, revisions, approval state, source freshness and historical provenance. Do not publish private history merely because it is exportable.\n\nRead `uiLanguage` from `contextory_context_get` and write new UI-facing record titles, descriptions, reasons, findings, decisions and outcomes in that VS Code language. Preserve existing/historical prose and exact IDs/paths/code. This does not replace the documentation corpus's required English/pt-BR pair: author both locales there, and let the UI choose its initial reading language.\n\nFor existing explanations, call `contextory_docs_get` with `section=sections` and a query, source path or local record ID. Page results, inspect source links and distinguish authored drafts, stale/unknown citations and approved requirements. The source graph remains the basis for definitions and dependencies. Use `Tools > Documentation`, Architecture's **Explain this area**, or a request's **Related documentation** for the human browser. Approvals remains the human knowledge review destination.\n\nBefore creating or migrating a corpus, read the bundled upstream playbooks through `contextory_docs_get section=guidance resource=...`. `offset` and `limit` page 400-character units; follow `nextOffset` to the end of the needed guide:\n\n- Create: `modules/software-documentation/skills/documentar-software/SKILL.md`.\n- Update/migrate: `modules/software-documentation/skills/atualizar-documentacao-software/SKILL.md`.\n- Corpus/schema and examples: `modules/software-documentation/references/corpus.md`.\n- Native source comments: `modules/software-documentation/internal-skills/documentar-codigo-fonte/SKILL.md`.\n- Consumer bundles/Pages: `modules/software-documentation/internal-skills/integrar-documentacao/SKILL.md`.\n- Carbon: `modules/carbon-design/internal-skills/guia-visual-carbon/SKILL.md`. List more tokens, component guides, templates and licenses with `section=guidance query=...`.\n\nThe upstream copies retain their original tool names and installation routing. In Contextory, use `contextory_docs_run action=...` instead of an external `docs_*` tool: `map`, `init`, `validate`, `build`, `integrations`, `migrate`, `readme`, `pages_plan`, `pages_publish`, `pages_status`. Project scope supplies the authorized root; do not pass an arbitrary workspace. Discover the advertised argument schema. Mutations require your current spec lease. `init` needs a name/kind, preserves existing docs and produces drafts. `migrate` and `readme` preview unless `apply=true`. Author the corpus with native file tools. A map/build does not write verified explanations for you or establish complete coverage.\n\nThe bundled generator creates schema 2 Carbon sites; retrieval also reads schema 1. Preserve existing content/IDs/translations/URLs/assets and preview migration before applying its backup-protected change. Contextory's own schema-1 documentation build is separately pinned and must not be silently upgraded as part of another feature. Write substantive paired English/pt-BR content and real source/API/UI evidence, with explicit coverage gaps. Use the local Carbon renderer and assets; do not substitute a generic theme.\n\nOperations return a durable job ID immediately. Poll `contextory_docs_get section=job jobId=...` (or `contextory_entity_get`) until terminal. Read large retained JSON artifacts with `contextory_project_export section=artifacts artifact=...`. A passed job means the operation ran; inspect validation/build results. A stale/invalid draft still needs work. Reuse the exact operation ID/input to recover an interrupted response, and inspect partially written output before starting a new job. No automatic mutation replay or publication occurs.\n\nAfter reviewing a section against source, use leased `contextory_docs_link` with its stable page/section IDs, current `corpusFingerprint`, relevant local record IDs and a reason. It records observed associations and citation hashes; later text/source changes mark the explanation stale. Preserve association audit history. This is maintenance evidence, not human approval. Keep affected explanations current as tracked work changes, and link outcome/decision/catalog records as appropriate.\n\nBuild/check locally and inspect responsive Carbon output, both locales, search, navigation, source coverage and consumer integration before delivery. A request to implement documentation does not itself authorize publishing a connected project's website. With existing publication authorization, inspect `pages_plan`, then run `pages_publish publish=true` with the selected remote and verify `pages_status`. Preserve native trust, unrelated configuration and work. Follow the Contextory workflow skill's independent checks, catalog accounting, outcome and explicit spec completion requirements for authorized implementation.\n",
  "contextory-migrate/SKILL.md": "---\nname: contextory-migrate\ndescription: Build a source-backed Contextory knowledge baseline for an existing project, including file responsibilities, rules, features, tests and interface contracts. Use when bootstrapping or resuming a project knowledge migration; ordinary code lookups do not need migration.\nmetadata:\n  version: \"3\"\n---\n\n<!-- contextory-managed-migrate -->\n\nAct as the project's migration agent in the native client. Use the advertised Contextory MCP capabilities; never start a second model service or execute project modules to infer behavior. Migrating knowledge does not authorize changing application behavior. Preserve source, existing approved records, client configuration and historical decisions.\n\nUse the project's `uiLanguage` from `contextory_context_get` for every new catalog title/description, finding, rationale, UI contract, review reason and report, even when chat uses another language. Preserve historical prose, exact identifiers, paths and code; reconstructed observations are not bulk translations of existing records.\n\nRead `contextory_context_get`, recover the migration request/spec through search and inspect its current state. For authorized migration, create missing tracked work, attach this session and claim/renew the spec lease. If the tools are unavailable, report the missing capability and retain a source-cited draft; do not claim the ledger was populated.\n\nUse `contextory_code_context_get` and inspect cited current source. Start `contextory_catalog_migrate` with `operationId`, `specId`, `leaseId` and a bounded `limit`. Resume with the returned `migration.id` in `migrationId` and a new operation ID, until `next` is null. After an interrupted call retry its exact operation ID/input, then inspect the run through `contextory_entity_get`. Its persisted manifest/cursor and skipped-file gaps are a file scan checkpoint, not semantic completion. Query `contextory_catalog_get` with `inventory:true` and page its independent record/proposal/inventory cursors to discover new or missing files after the scan. A later scan preserves existing descriptions.\n\nWork through subsystems in bounded batches. Read file bodies, entry points, callers, docs, tests and earlier decisions. Replace generated symbol lists with concise explanations of actual file responsibility through `contextory_catalog_change` revision proposals. Record unknowns instead of inventing purpose. Register distinct rule and feature candidates with their source references and specific test file/symbol links, or `testGap` explaining missing validation. Reconcile contradictory implementation, documentation and tests as findings; describe observed implementation separately from intended behavior.\n\nFor every interface surface, inspect its layout and event/state handlers, and inspect the running UI when available and authorized. Add `category:\"ui\"` contracts with `ui.location`, `element`, `trigger`, `behavior` and `states`: placement/grouping, control/value, click/change/keyboard trigger, resulting action/state/persistence/navigation, and relevant initial/loading/empty/error/disabled states. Link behavioral tests or explicit gaps. Static extraction cannot prove pixel layout, dynamic dispatch or runtime effects; mark those limits with sources and follow-up findings. Projects without a UI should record why this category is inapplicable in the migration report.\n\nUse leased `contextory_catalog_change` creates for new observations. Compatible corrections to file-purpose descriptions/source links can use `compatibility:preserved`, `impact:implementation`, `autoApprove:true`, the current revision and a source-backed reason; observations remain unapproved. Behavioral descriptions and UI contracts stay exact through auto-approval, even on observations. Implementation conflicts may auto-approve with a recorded rationale, but conflicts with process rules or results, changed behavior and retirements need a before/after human proposal before implementation. Link historical decisions/specs through `decisionIds` or `contextory_relationship_change`. Generated facts and legacy decisions never become retroactive human approval. Optional baseline review does not block migration. Wait on actual pending proposals; never borrow human credentials, infer approval from silence, or erase superseded/rejected history.\n\nBefore finalizing, reconcile the current inventory, stale/missing source fingerprints, duplicate descriptions, pending proposals, rule/feature-to-test gaps and interface interactions against the scope. Record a source-cited migration report explaining reviewed subsystems, omissions, conflicts, test gaps and categories not applicable. Keep resumable outstanding work when review is pending. Do not describe a completed file scan as a complete application specification. Run configured meaningful independent checks, map evidence to each criterion, include catalog IDs/explanation in the outcome, explicitly complete only verified specs and export the request report. No new application tests are needed merely to restate documentation.\n\nFor later documentation requests, use `contextory_project_export` overview and page the needed datasets (including immutable revisions, decisions, catalog entries/proposals, relationships, checks and artifacts). Use `contextory_catalog_get` for freshness and `contextory_entity_get` for exact revisions. Cite sequence/snapshot, approved versus observed state, evidence origin and coverage limits; omit superseded behavior from current instructions while retaining it in historical documentation.\n",
  "contextory-workflow/references/approvals-and-regressions.md": "<!-- contextory-managed-resource -->\n\n# Approvals and regression investigation\n\nReview cards ask for three natural-language explanations, supplied by the agent as `explanation.change`, `explanation.approvalReason` and `explanation.behavior` in the VS Code UI language. The user can approve/reject without a comment, or request changes with instructions. A `changes_requested` proposal still blocks implementation/completion; revise its same ID and current revision under its original spec, update the explanation, and wait for another decision. Automatic source snapshots require no user action or comment, never approve behavior and retain prior hashes. Missing references and edits racing with approval remain explicit blockers; the UI reloads automatically.\n\nThe lease's exact provider/native session is retained on the proposal and spec. Replies are durable, project-scoped audit events returned only to that session's trusted hook. The launcher waits within a four-minute budget; timeout/outage never approves. Hook delivery acknowledges transport, not agent execution. Unknown identities remain unbound and require manual recovery. Claude can reopen a saved session through its installed extension command; Codex resumes the saved conversation in an interactive terminal because no documented targeted extension command is available. Bob/generic clients receive a copyable session ID. A stopped native client cannot be woken through a nonexistent public API. Keep pending responses and never redirect them to a different active conversation.\n\nClassify the effect on established behavior before implementation. Replacing function A with B while preserving results, errors, side effects and interface behavior needs no new human decision. Maintain its source/test references with `contextory_catalog_change action=revise`, the current `expectedRevision`, `compatibility=preserved`, `impact=implementation`, `autoApprove=true` and a concrete `reason` explaining compatibility and validation. The catalog keeps before/after definitions and hashes, spec, actor, rationale and review mode. An approved behavioral definition keeps its exact description/UI contract and original human review; an observed baseline stays observed. File-purpose prose may be corrected for implementation changes. Changed source still needs meaningful executed checks before completion.\n\nAn implementation conflict can also use `autoApprove=true`, `compatibility=conflicting`, `impact=implementation` when it does not change a process rule or the outcome. Explain which implementation choices conflict, why the chosen resolution preserves behavior, affected sources/tests and validation. Link relevant prior `decisionIds`. Conflicts involving rule entries, process/outcome or unclassified decisions, changed approved behavior, retirements and uncertain conflicts require human review. Request scope/native permissions continue to apply.\n\nPrepare these review proposals before editing the disputed behavior. Keep the established contract effective until the exact proposal is approved in Approvals. Pending proposals persist across service/session restarts and block implementation tools and Stop across the project; reads, catalog preparation, independent checks and lease renewal remain available. Use the native question/wait handoff, not a polling loop or another shell/tool. Hook status failures block implementation/completion until recovery. Generic MCP has no native file-tool hook; the service still blocks workflow mutations/completion, and the agent must honor the wait. Hooks do not intercept editors or unreported changes. Native hook trust and reload remain host-owned.\n\nAfter approval, implement and validate. If sources changed since a proposal was prepared, a human must inspect and refresh its exact snapshot before approval. After rejection, retain or restore the established behavior, record the resolution and validate before continuing. Human review of a baseline is optional; do not stop migration or ordinary work merely because observations are not approved.\n\nWhen recording a decision, set `impact`. `contextory_decision_record supersedes=...` auto-reviews only implementation-to-implementation conflicts. For a process/outcome or unclassified prior decision, prepare a catalog proposal in the current spec with that decision in `decisionIds` and its replacement choice as the exact `content.description`. After human approval, pass its `approvalId`. Earlier project decisions and the explicit supersedes edge remain available across specs. Never relabel historical uncertainty as human approval.\n\nFor a report that something used to work:\n\n1. Recover the affected feature/rule/UI contract and current source using code context, catalog and documentation. Record the observed failure and a reproducer as a finding; distinguish expected behavior from the current symptom.\n2. Use impact/path queries and search the feature, source path/symbol, finding, catalog ID and related decisions. Page history and read exact revisions, including auto-approved changes, reasons, before/after hashes, spec and prior passing evidence.\n3. Compare the last known working version/commit and current source. Use version history for exact Git membership. A ledger time interval or static relationship identifies candidates, not proven causation. Check callers, dependencies, interface states, errors and side effects; use Git history/bisect where useful and authorized.\n4. Reproduce the cause, add a focused regression check, fix it, and link the finding/fix to the responsible change/spec/source using `contextory_relationship_change`. Record uncertainty if causation is not established. Update affected catalog/documentation and submit current independent evidence with the outcome.\n\nKeep all material changes indexed even when no human review is needed. Auto-approval is an attributed implementation decision, never proof of passing tests or a human-approved requirement.\n",
  "contextory-workflow/references/completion.md": "<!-- contextory-managed-resource -->\n\n# Completion and evidence\n\nMulti-project work is a coordinated group of project-owned requests, not a shared ledger or lease. Carry the same projectId (and workspaceId when needed) through each branch's session, spec lease, findings, catalog, checks, evidence, outcome, completion and report export. Evidence and record IDs from another project cannot complete a branch. After every local request completes, query contextory_workspace_get with groupId; offline, detached or missing branches remain explicitly incomplete. Recover partial creation with its exact original operationId/input; each branch retains that input. Native hook/model/usage observations are not reassigned across projects by inference.\n\nRecover earlier work with focused search and read its current request/spec state before creating records. Read cited historical revisions for rationale, then check whether later decisions supersede them. Keep observations, generated source relationships and approved criteria distinct. Hooks can restore reminders after compaction but do not carry the full current contract; reread `contextory_context_get` for it.\n\nAttach a Contextory session with `contextory_session_attach`; never guess a native session ID. Claim/renew with `contextory_spec_lease`. Mutations require fresh revisions and unique operation IDs. Read the record after a conflict rather than replaying a changed mutation with an old idempotency key.\n\nRecord issues through `contextory_finding_record`, explaining impact, resolution and relevant sources. Blocking findings must be resolved before completion. Record concise choices and tradeoffs through `contextory_decision_record`. These explanations are project records, not chat transcripts.\n\nUse `contextory_check_run` recipes and poll with `contextory_entity_get`. With no recipes, configure the project's existing meaningful check command in workspace `contextory.checks`, then reread context. If no meaningful check is available, report verification as pending. Agent-reported external results remain reported evidence and cannot substitute for an independent runner's passing result.\n\nAn outcome maps every acceptance criterion exactly once to current evidence. Finish source/configuration edits before final independent checks: evidence is bound to the source fingerprint, contract and attempt, so later changes may require new checks. A static test-reference edge is not test execution evidence. Explain changes, issues/fixes, decisions, validation and remaining limits, including when there were no findings or new decisions. Submit the outcome, explicitly complete the spec with the returned revision, and confirm its state. Export the full request using `contextory_report_export`.\n\nUse only advertised workflow mutations. If a required workflow tool or connection is unavailable, preserve the work and explicitly report which tracking or verification step remains pending; native source inspection can continue within the authorized scope. Never pretend an unavailable call succeeded or that a hook completed a spec.\n\nWhen a project catalog exists, include `catalog:{recordIds,explanation}` in the outcome. Explain additions, human-reviewed behavior changes, agent auto-approved maintenance/conflicts, retirements or why behavior is unchanged. Pending project proposals and stale affected approved source/test references block submission/completion. Refresh compatible source links through the leased auto-approval path, retaining the approved contract and original review; a changed hash alone is not a changed requirement. Observed baselines have optional review. A definition revision after submission invalidates its accounted outcome. Human review remains in Approvals for process/outcome conflicts and changed behavior; never borrow UI credentials. Page `contextory_project_export` for the complete stored base and immutable revisions. Follow [approvals and regression investigation](approvals-and-regressions.md) when classifying changes or tracing failures.\n",
  "contextory-workflow/SKILL.md": "---\nname: contextory-workflow\ndescription: Track authorized software implementation in the connected Contextory companion. Use for requests, milestones, specs, issues, design decisions, evidence and continuing tracked work; ordinary questions need no new request.\nmetadata:\n  version: \"10\"\n---\n\n<!-- contextory-managed-workflow -->\n\nFor changes requiring human review, always supply `catalog_change.explanation` in the project's UI language: `change` explains what changes, `approvalReason` explains why a decision is needed, and `behavior` explains the application behavior affected. Use natural language. Sources refresh automatically without a human comment or approval; immutable hashes and before/after definitions remain in history. Humans approve/reject without required text, or send instructions with **Request changes**. For `changes_requested`, read `data.feedback` and revise that same proposal ID at its current revision under the original spec, updating its content and explanation. Wait again for approval; feedback never authorizes implementation. Attach the exact hook-supplied `nativeSessionId` to route replies to that conversation. Trusted native hooks can wait up to four minutes and return replies there; expiry still blocks. Idle/stopped chats may need **Reopen conversation** or manual resume. Never guess an identity or send to an arbitrary active chat.\n\nRequires the workflow tools advertised by a connected Contextory MCP server. Conversation, editing, model selection and permissions remain in the native client; the workflow is shared across agents. Ordinary questions need no new tracked work.\n\nRead `uiLanguage` and `contentLanguageInstruction` from `contextory_context_get` before writing records. Write all new UI-facing prose in that VS Code language (`en` or `pt-BR`): titles, descriptions, criteria, findings/fixes, decisions/rationale, review reasons, catalog/UI contracts, outcomes and reports, even if this conversation uses another language. Preserve historical content and exact IDs, paths, symbols, code, commands and protocol keys. Keep documentation's required paired locales. Do not bulk-translate existing records; headless projects default to English unless an editor configured their language.\n\nWhen managed hooks are missing, stale or duplicated, advertised `contextory_hooks_rebuild` repairs only the chosen project's native hooks and launcher. Pass explicit `projectId`, native `provider` and a new `operationId`; no spec lease is needed for this setup repair. Follow its trust/reload guidance and verify an observed hook event. Do not repeatedly rebuild an unchanged setup or edit global trust records. If MCP is unavailable, use the editor's **Contextory: Rebuild Agent Hooks** command and inspect service logs; native approval remains client-owned. See the codebase skill's recovery guidance for Codex workspace path casing.\n\nFor multiple workspace folders, read `contextory_workspace_get` and keep an explicit `projectId` on every related workflow/source call, adding `workspaceId` for ambiguous editor contexts. Each project owns its requests, records, sessions/leases, catalog, checks, evidence and releases. Never infer project identity from an ID, source path or the UI selector. For user-authorized work spanning projects, `contextory_workspace_request_create` creates linked local requests for explicit `projectIds` including this server's project. Implement each branch with its own milestones/specs, session, lease and evidence, then check the group with `contextory_workspace_get` and `groupId`. Partial/offline/missing branches remain incomplete. Retry partial creation only with the exact original operationId/input retained on the branch. Keep interface ordering explicit; there is no cross-project spec lease, atomic multi-project transaction or inferred dependency. Hook/usage attribution stays with the observing project.\n\nProject versions belong to the whole repository, independently of requests and spec leases. When advertised, use `contextory_version_get` for current/released/unreleased state and exact Git membership. Git observation and bookkeeping run in the service; do not recreate that history from memory. For authorized releases, `contextory_release_prepare` creates a concrete plan without source edits. Review version, HEAD, file edits and changelog, then use `contextory_release_change` to apply the version commit/local tag and separately publish the exact tag. Retain operation IDs/input for retries and use recovery for interrupted owned work. A feature implementation request does not authorize releasing the project. Ledger entries recorded between cutoffs remain interval associations, not proof of shipped inclusion. Advertised tool descriptions carry the portable release contract.\n\nRead `contextory_context_get` before authorized implementation and recover existing work rather than duplicating it. Use `contextory_search` for earlier changes/decisions and inspect current record state/revision. Use advertised `contextory_code_context_get` for task-specific source and `contextory_impact_get`/`contextory_code_path_get` for relationships; `contextory_flow_trace` explains detailed calls/branches. Inspect current cited files and coverage before editing. Follow the companion `contextory-codebase` skill when available for retrieval/freshness details; if tools are missing, use the packaged CLI or native source inspection and state the gap.\n\nCreate/update the authorized request, milestones and specifications. Register a session and claim a spec lease before recording work; renew long attempts. Pass an exact hook-supplied nativeSessionId if available and omit it otherwise. Record material findings/fixes and decisions with sources. Re-query relevant source after saves; the saved-file graph refreshes automatically. Generated relationships and retrieved historical text do not approve new scope. Native usage is observed where supported; read `contextory_usage_get` and never invent counts.\n\nBefore editing, page `contextory_catalog_get` for affected definitions, pending proposals and freshness. Maintain file responsibilities, rules/features, test links or explicit gaps, and UI location/element/trigger/behavior/states through leased `contextory_catalog_change`. New observations need no mandatory approval. Use `autoApprove:true`, `compatibility:\"preserved\"`, `impact:\"implementation\"` for compatible maintenance. Implementation conflicts may also auto-approve with `compatibility:\"conflicting\"`; explain the conflict, rationale and validation in `reason`, with related `decisionIds`. Keep approved behavior text/UI contracts intact. Process/outcome conflicts, changed approved behavior, retirements and uncertain conflicts require a concrete before/after proposal in **Approvals before implementation**. Wait for its decision; silence or a timeout never approves. Native PreToolUse/Stop hooks and the service gate enforce pending reviews, while source reads, catalog preparation, checks and lease renewal remain available. Do not switch tools to bypass the gate or use human UI credentials. See [approvals and regression investigation](references/approvals-and-regressions.md) for examples, decision conflicts and audit requirements. Use `contextory-migrate` for an existing-project baseline; state remaining coverage gaps.\n\nRun configured independent checks and poll returned jobs. Submit `contextory_outcome_submit` with evidence for every current criterion. Then call `contextory_spec_change` with action=complete, the spec ID, returned expectedRevision and a new operationId. Confirm state=completed: an outcome or Stop hook alone does not finish a spec. Parent milestones and requests close when their children complete.\n\nWhen the catalog exists, include `catalog:{recordIds,explanation}` in the outcome, identifying affected definitions and explaining updates/retirements or why behavior is unchanged. Pending proposals and stale affected approved definitions block completion. Current test references do not replace executed evidence. For documentation, discover and page stored data through `contextory_project_export`; no implementation request is needed for a read-only export.\n\nResolve or report explicit completion blockers. Keep native permissions and authorized scope intact. Read [completion and evidence](references/completion.md) for leases, checks, findings and reports.\n"
};
